      overview: "Explains what form Musharakah capital should take, why debt cannot count as capital, and how capital commingling works in principle even when it isn't physically pooled.",
      simpleExplanation: "Musharakah capital should ideally be money (or its clear value if it's goods), and everyone agrees an unpaid debt can't count as capital until it's actually collected. The idea of 'commingling' capital doesn't mean everyone's money has to be physically dumped into one pile - it can be done on paper (constructively), based on an agreed valuation, so each partner's ownership share in the joint venture is clear.",
      academicExplanation: "The majority of jurists (particularly Maliki, Hanbali, Shafi'e) hold that partner capital should be liquid assets (money/currency) with unambiguously known value; Hanafis, however, don't require the capital amount to be known at CONTRACT time - it can be agreed before business commencement. Capital must not be a debt or a nonexistent commodity. Al-Sarakhsi notes capital forms vary by local 'Urf (custom); Al-Kasani says the matter follows local investment practice (currency or goods). Contemporary jurists are unanimous that goods-based capital's VALUE must be assessed in monetary terms; debt cannot become partnership capital until actually RECEIVED. In limited companies, capital takes the form of equal 'shares' units, purchasable disproportionately by intended partners. Objects not sellable/usable under Islamic law cannot form partnership capital. Shirkah rules broadly require capital 'merger and commingling' - meaning individual ownership is replaced by collective joint-venture ownership, with any Musharakah asset value appreciation reflecting ALL partners' rights per their capital ratio (a partner cannot claim sole entitlement to appreciation of a specific asset, e.g. a shop, he personally contributed). Commingling does NOT require indistinguishable cash/identical goods or actual transfer at contract time - the merger can be ACTUAL or CONSTRUCTIVE (the latter based on agreed-standard valuation, e.g. market value); for goods, partner shares are calculated by their money value at contract time. Usmani's synthesis: Musharakah share capital can be contributed in cash OR commodities, with commodities' market value determining the partner's capital share. Other modern capital-related aspects: a person can become a partner of a running business (with fixed assets) by investing cash/kind capital; merger of various partnership firms is also possible. This implies all partners' capital must be quantified/specified - for a running business, cash/receivables are valued at face value, currency conversion uses the Musharakah contract execution date's rate, fixed assets use agreed valuation, and for running Musharakah (e.g. Islamic bank deposit management via Shirkah), the AVERAGE utilized amount is treated as Musharakah capital.",
      examExplanation: "Musharakah capital: majority view (Maliki, Hanbali, Shafi'e) requires liquid/known-value capital; Hanafi view allows the exact amount to be agreed BEFORE business commencement (not necessarily at contract signing). Capital cannot be a debt (until actually received) or a nonexistent commodity. Commingling = individual ownership replaced by COLLECTIVE ownership (so asset appreciation benefits all partners per capital ratio, not whoever contributed that specific asset) - can be ACTUAL (physical transfer) or CONSTRUCTIVE (agreed valuation, e.g. market value), with goods-based capital always valued in monetary terms. For running Musharakah (e.g. bank deposit pools), the AVERAGE utilized amount serves as Musharakah capital.",
      keyPoints: [
        "The majority view (Maliki, Hanbali, Shafi'e) requires Musharakah capital to be liquid assets with unambiguously known value at contract time; the Hanafi view allows the exact capital amount to be agreed before business commencement, rather than strictly at contract signing.",
        "Debt cannot become partnership capital until it is actually received; a nonexistent commodity also cannot serve as capital.",
        "'Commingling' of capital does not require physical, indistinguishable pooling - the merger can be constructive, based on an agreed valuation standard (e.g. market value), with goods-based capital always assessed in monetary terms.",
        "Because commingled capital means COLLECTIVE ownership, any appreciation in a Musharakah asset's value benefits ALL partners per their capital ratio - a partner cannot claim sole entitlement to the appreciation of a specific asset he personally contributed.",
        "For a running Musharakah (e.g. an Islamic bank's deposit-based Shirkah pool), the AVERAGE utilized amount over the period is treated as the Musharakah capital for profit-sharing purposes."
      ],
      definitions: [
        { term: "Commingling", definition: "The Shirkah requirement that partner capital be merged such that individual ownership is replaced by collective joint-venture ownership; the merger may be actual (physical) or constructive (based on an agreed valuation standard)." }
      ],
      conditions: [
        "Musharakah capital must not be a debt (until actually received) or a nonexistent commodity.",
        "Goods-based capital contributions must have their market value assessed in monetary terms to determine each partner's capital share."
      ],
      principles: [],
      processSteps: [],
      examples: [],
      commonConfusions: [
        "Students sometimes think 'commingling' requires literally pooling identical cash or goods together at contract signing. The chapter clarifies this can be done constructively, via agreed valuation, without requiring physical transfer or indistinguishable pooling at that exact moment."
      ],
      importantDistinctions: [
        "Actual commingling (physical merger of capital) vs constructive commingling (based on agreed valuation, e.g. market value) - both are valid ways to satisfy the capital-merger requirement."
      ],
