      overview: "Walks through the full Diminishing Musharakah procedure used for housing finance, contrasts it with the trade-financing variant (where only the bank's promise to sell is binding), and details the documentation sequence and sale-and-lease-back rules for construction/renovation.",
      simpleExplanation: "For fixed-asset financing (like buying a house), the bank and client jointly own the asset; the client rents the bank's share and gradually buys it out unit by unit, with rent shrinking as the client's ownership grows. For a TRADE business (not a physical asset with rent), it works differently: only the BANK's promise to eventually sell its stake is binding - the client-entrepreneur is never forced to actually buy it, and any sale happens at a fair market valuation, not a fixed price. In practice, this all happens through a careful sequence of separate legal documents: first a partnership agreement, then a rental agreement, then a separate purchase undertaking - each one signed independently.",
      academicExplanation: "DM can easily finance fixed assets for Islamic banks: house financing, auto financing, plant/machinery financing, factory/building financing, and other fixed-asset financing. For HOUSING FINANCE specifically: joint ownership is created; the financier partner leases his undivided share to the using partner; the client pays rent on the financier's part and PERIODICALLY purchases units of the financier's ownership. The Shari'ah-approved modus operandi requires THREE SEPARATE contracts, each independent of the other two, in this SEQUENCE: (1) a contract between partners creating joint ownership - the client partner makes a promise (before or after the lease agreement is finalized) to purchase the financier partner's share; (2) the financing partner gives units of his share to the client ON LEASE; (3) the client partner progressively purchases the financing partner's ownership units per his promise, with RENT correspondingly decreasing. Financing forms VARY by asset type: SOME assets can be leased (house financing, plant/machinery purchase); assets of a COMMERCIAL nature typically don't involve leasing. Diminishing Musharakah in Trade (12.9.1): if leasing is NOT involved and it's a simple business partnership (e.g. 40:60 split), the partners can agree that one partner's share units will be PERIODICALLY SOLD to the other, who keeps buying until the first party exits entirely. Since this contract exists for PROFIT-EARNING and involves NO leasing (unlike house/vehicle DM), the share-unit PRICE CANNOT be fixed in the promise to sell - one partner may instead agree to sell based on BUSINESS VALUATION at each unit-purchase time, per recognized expert principles (with the expert's identity agreed when the promise is signed); at actual purchase time, the sale must be executed via proper offer and acceptance. Although the entrepreneur partner has an inherent motivation to acquire full ownership, Shari'ah experts are NOT inclined to make the PURCHASE binding on him. Per OIC Fiqh Academy Resolution No. 2 (Jeddah) and IRTI research, the sale provision can be made binding ONLY on the FINANCIER partner, with the sale effected at PREVAILING MARKET PRICES at the time of actual sale. After creating joint ownership, the bank may sign a ONE-SIDED promise to sell different ownership-share units periodically, undertaking that as the client purchases a unit, remaining-unit rental will decrease accordingly - the bank makes a BINDING promise to offer a specific ownership portion for sale on a specified future date, at a price DETERMINED AT ACTUAL SALE TIME; the entrepreneur partner may VOLUNTARILY buy the financier's share at prevailing stock market prices or a freely-consented price. Procedure and Documentation (12.9.2) for housing finance via Shirkatulmilk-based DM: (1) Joint ownership creation via a Musharakah agreement - customer and IFI become co-owners; if the customer ALREADY holds legal title, the agreement instead has the IFI acquiring a certain Musha'a share, involving a SALE-AND-LEASE-BACK arrangement. (2) Rent agreement - both parties agree the IFI will lease its undivided share to the client for stipulated rental, governed by Ijarah rules; this is signed AFTER the Musharakah agreement, detailing rent, calculation formula, and lease-period schedule. (3) Undertaking to purchase units of the bank's share - a UNILATERAL promise binding ONLY on the promisor (either the client or the bank can make it); for Shirkatulmilk arrangements, it may include a PRICE SCHEDULE for periodic unit purchase, provisions for accelerated purchase, and security/guarantee details (normally equitable mortgage of the financed property, plus possibly additional security given the client's financial position). The customer thus pays rent for using the bank's property share while progressively purchasing part of that share until full ownership transfers - usable for buying, building, or renovating a house, or replacing an existing interest-based housing loan (a 'balance transfer facility'), and other fixed assets can similarly be DM-financed. For construction on the customer's OWN land or renovation (Box 12.4): this involves a purchase/SALE-AND-LEASE-BACK structure - the bank purchases a PART of the customer's land/property (forming Shirkatulmilk joint ownership) using proceeds the customer then uses for construction/renovation; once complete/habitable, the bank leases its share to the client; crucially, the bank's units are NOT sold back to the client until a ONE-YEAR gap after the LAST disbursement instalment, specifically to avoid Bai' al 'Inah (prohibited buy-back) - during this initial no-sale period, the client pays ONLY rent (which does not decrease), and only after the one-year gap does the unit-selling (and corresponding rent decrease) begin. If the client regularly pays rental and periodically purchases the bank's share, ownership fully transfers over time; if he delays, rental simply doesn't decrease (the bank's income loss is far LESS severe than in Murabaha or even simple leasing, since the customer keeps paying rent on the bank-owned units regardless). The client CAN purchase multiple units at once if cash flow allows - in which case banks typically conduct a VALUATION and share any capital-gain appreciation (up to a pre-stipulated rate, e.g. 3-4%) with the client, giving the client the remainder - this built-in valuation-sharing mechanism provides risk mitigation for the bank. Rental rates can be fixed or floating, subject to a proper floor-and-cap to avoid Gharar (which could otherwise render the transaction non-Shari'ah-compliant).",
      examExplanation: "Housing DM procedure (3 sequential, independent contracts): (1) joint ownership + client's promise to purchase; (2) financier leases share to client (Ijarah rules); (3) client progressively buys financier's units per the promise, rent decreases correspondingly. Trade DM: NO leasing involved; price CANNOT be pre-fixed (business VALUATION at each purchase instead); ONLY the FINANCIER's promise to sell is BINDING (per OIC Fiqh Academy Resolution No. 2/IRTI) - the entrepreneur is NEVER obligated to buy, sale at PREVAILING MARKET price. Construction-on-own-land/renovation (Box 12.4): sale-and-lease-back structure; bank buys PART of customer's land/property; ONE-YEAR gap required after LAST disbursement before bank starts selling units back (avoids Bai' al 'Inah) - during that year, client pays ONLY rent (no decrease). Multi-unit purchases: bank shares capital-gain appreciation (up to a pre-stipulated cap %) with the client as a built-in risk-mitigation feature.",
