    "The central Musharakah rule: profit-sharing ratio is freely negotiable by agreement (can reflect work contribution), but loss must always be shared exactly per capital investment ratio - the maxim 'profit is based on agreement, loss is always subject to the ratio of investment.'",
    "Mudarabah differs from Musharakah on seven key dimensions: investment source, management rights, loss-bearing, liability scope, profit distribution timing, asset ownership, and dissolution valuation requirements.",
    "Modern joint stock companies are treated as Shirkah al 'Inan with limited liability as the key exception; preference shares are not Shari'ah-compliant, and share tradability depends on whether company capital remains liquid (face value only) or has converted to nonliquid assets (any agreed market price).",
    "Diminishing Musharakah requires its constituent subcontracts (partnership, lease, sale) to be entered separately and remain legally independent, never conditional on one another.",
    "The critical Diminishing Musharakah distinction: in Shirkatulmilk-based DM (e.g. housing), a pre-agreed purchase price IS permissible; in Shirkatul'aqd-based DM (e.g. trade), the price cannot be pre-fixed, and only the financier's promise to sell is binding.",
